I was thinking that there should be better primitives for referencing
IO address than just &CAR and &RPLACA.  The trouble is that you
can have an untyped pointer lying around and then get a GC which
can get pretty confused.  Perhaps a (&read-as-type <type>|0 non-pointer|addr)
which takes &car of addr, and does a &set-type as an atomic operation.

For writes, it might be nice to have (&32-bit-write non-pointer|addr <?>|x <?>|y)
which stores low-8-bits(x):pointer(y) into addr.  This requires the
ability to move bits from the low part of some register into the type
part of the bus.

You could also have the inverse (&32-bit-read <type>|0 non-pointer|addr)
which returns the contents of the location's high 8 bits, address as a
CONS of two typed goodies.  (This requires the inverse data path capability).

Also, have you thought of what is needed if you want to run more than one
SCHIP communicating?  It seems to me that they will have to have
completely disjoint heaps, because of the unfriendly nature of the GC mark,
so that there is no way of passing objects other than numbers
between the processors.  I suspect there is some way of replacing
the top-level of a SCHIP with a PV type operation so that when a GC
is needed, all SCHIPs place themselves on a queue waiting for all to
become idle, and then the first guy that sees that everbody is waiting
seizes a lock and does the GC.
   
